## Versioning and Authority

The HyperMarkDown language follows Semantic Versioning (SemVer 2.0.0) and is
versioned independently of its software implementations. Editorial corrections
and clarifications that do not change the syntax or semantics of the language
are published as patch releases.

This specification is the authoritative definition of HyperMarkDown.
Implementations and accompanying documentation are derived from the standard,
rather than defining it.

## Terms

| Term | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| **card** | A card is the fundamental unit of knowledge in HyperMarkDown, represented as an individually addressable document. Cards within a namespace can be interconnected to form a structured knowledge base. |
| **HyperMarkDown card** | A card with the extension `.hmd`; the only kind that is scanned |
| **Markdown card** | A card with the extension `.md` or `.markdown`: a link and embed target, never scanned |
| **vault** | The directory that contains a `.hmd/` directory; it holds the configuration |
| **namespace root** | The directory inside the vault where the cards start, named by the `wiki` setting |
| **namespace** | Every card below the namespace root: the tree a reference resolves in |
| **module** | A directory inside the namespace; the unit that scopes a name |
| **folder note** | A card named `index`, standing for its directory |
| **construct** | A syntactic element defined by the HyperMarkDown language standard |
| **wikilink** | A link in double square brackets, such as `[[setup]]`, optionally with display text after a pipe |
| **embed** | A wikilink with `!` in front, expanded in place |
| **block anchor** | A trailing `^id` that names a block |
| **fragment** | What follows `#` in a target: a heading's text, or `^` and a block anchor's identifier |
| **unqualified reference** | A target with no leading `/`, `./`, or `../`, which is searched for |
| **spine** | The card's own directory and every directory above it, up to the namespace root |
| **sweep** | The search of the whole namespace that runs when nothing nearer matched; also called autodiscovery |
| **key** | The normalised form of a heading's text, used to match a fragment |
| **cell** | A fenced block that a tool runs or draws |

## The example vault

The sections below use one small vault. It is real: the lint output shown is
what the canonical implementation prints for it.

```text
handbook/                      the vault
  .hmd/config.toml             wiki = "wiki"
  wiki/                        the namespace root
    index.hmd                  folder note of the root, published
    setup.hmd                  # Setup, ## Linux ^setup-linux, ## macOS
    guides/                    a module
      index.hmd                folder note of guides/
      install.hmd
      tokens.hmd
    reference/                 a module
      tokens.hmd
      api.md                   a Markdown card
```

`guides/install.hmd`:

```markdown
---
import:
  - from /reference import api
---

# Install

Follow [[setup#linux]], or embed it:

![[setup#^setup-linux]]

Every request carries one of the [[tokens]]; the endpoints are in [[api]].
Deploying is described in [[deploy]].
```

How each of its references resolves:

| Reference | Result | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `[[setup#linux]]` | `setup.hmd`, the heading `Linux` | Found on the spine, at the root; the fragment matches by key |
| `![[setup#^setup-linux]]` | the `Linux` section of `setup.hmd` | An anchor on a heading line embeds that heading's section |
| `[[tokens]]` | `guides/tokens.hmd` | The nearest directory on the spine wins |
| `[[api]]` | `reference/api.md` | A named import binds the name |
| `[[deploy]]` | unresolved: a warning | A forward reference, not a failure |

`setup.hmd` also links `[[tokens]]`. Its spine is the root alone, which holds no
`tokens`, so the sweep runs and finds two cards. `hmd lint` reports:

```text
guides/install.hmd:13:27: warning[HMD001] [[deploy]] does not resolve to a page
setup.hmd:9:51: error[HMD002] [[tokens]] matches 2 pages; qualify it (candidates: guides/tokens.hmd, reference/tokens.hmd)

1 error(s), 1 warning(s)
```

## Conformance to the Standard

Independent implementations of HyperMarkDown must interpret documents according
to the syntax and semantics defined by this standard. Given the same document
and vault context, implementations must recognize the same language constructs,
resolve references to the same targets, and assign the same meaning to those
constructs.

The key words **MUST**, **MUST NOT**, **REQUIRED**, **SHOULD**, **SHOULD NOT**,
and **MAY** are to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119.

- A **conforming implementation** MUST accept every document this specification
  defines as well-formed, MUST assign it the meaning given here, and MUST report
  each condition listed under [Diagnostics](#diagnostics) with the identifier
  and severity given there.
- An implementation MAY provide constructs beyond this specification, but MUST
  NOT assign a different meaning to any construct defined here.
- **CommonMark compatibility.** Every document CommonMark 0.31.2 accepts is
  syntactically valid HyperMarkDown. CommonMark constructs keep their CommonMark
  meaning, except where their bytes form a HyperMarkDown construct. Where this
  document and CommonMark 0.31.2 disagree about inherited syntax, this document
  is right; whatever inherited syntax it does not restate is specified at
  <https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/>.
- **What must agree with CommonMark in 0.1.** An implementation MUST find the
  constructs where a CommonMark reading of the document puts them, within the
  [known divergence](#lexical-model). How it renders the inherited Markdown is
  not part of conformance in this version.
- **Determinism.** The same tree MUST produce the same result — the same
  resolutions, the same expansions, and byte-identical diagnostics — however
  often it is processed, on whichever platform, and in whatever order its files
  were created or are listed by the filesystem.
- **Arbitration.** Where two implementations disagree about behaviour this
  document leaves under-determined, the language-neutral conformance corpus at
  `examples/conformance/cases/` decides. A case that starts passing or failing
  unexpectedly is a defect in the implementation, not in the corpus.

Renaming a `.md` file to `.hmd` keeps it syntactically valid, but can change
how it is read: `[[foo]]` is literal text in CommonMark and a wikilink in
HyperMarkDown, and a leading `---` block that matches the grammar below is
frontmatter.

**Why.** Rendering is left out of conformance in 0.1 because the renderers in
use, this project's site included, keep older Markdown rules. The constructs
are what HyperMarkDown owns, so they are what has to agree.

## Documents

A card is stored as a file with a recognized card extension.
The card extensions, in priority order, are exactly:

```text
.hmd  .md  .markdown
```

- A `.hmd` file is a **HyperMarkDown card**: an OPTIONAL frontmatter block
  followed by CommonMark content. A card with no frontmatter and no
  HyperMarkDown construct is well-formed. Where this specification says "card"
  without saying what it contains, it means a HyperMarkDown card.
- A `.md` or `.markdown` file is a **Markdown card**, specified under
  [Markdown cards](#markdown-cards).
- Where two files compete for one name, the earlier extension wins; see
  [Binding](#binding-an-unqualified-reference-in-one-directory).
- A file is a member of the namespace if and only if it lies inside the
  [namespace root](#the-namespace) and has a card extension. Files with any
  other extension MUST be ignored: they are neither link targets nor inputs to
  checking.

### Encoding

- A card MUST be encoded in UTF-8. A file with a card extension that is not
  valid UTF-8 MUST be reported as an error at line 1, column 1 ([HMD019](#diagnostics)). It is
  then not a card: it is neither scanned nor a link target.
- A single byte-order mark (`EF BB BF`) at the start of a card MUST be
  ignored. Byte 0 of the card, wherever this specification names it, is the
  first byte after it.
- A line ends at `\n`, at `\r\n`, or at a `\r` not followed by `\n`, as in
  CommonMark.
- A name in a reference and a name on disk MUST be compared after both are
  normalised to Unicode NFC.

**Why.** One encoding keeps every tool, and git, reading cards the same way. A
byte-order mark is invisible, and if it were not ignored it would hide
frontmatter. The same name can be stored in two Unicode forms, and a reference
must resolve the same way on every platform.

### Markdown cards

A Markdown card is addressable as a card and is read as CommonMark.

- A Markdown card MUST NOT be scanned. Its body yields no wikilink, no embed and
  no block anchor; a `[[...]]` in it is text.
- It MAY open with a frontmatter block under the same fence rule. An
  implementation MUST expose the mapping and MUST NOT interpret any of its keys,
  the four reserved keys included. A block that does not parse as a YAML
  mapping is body text, not an error.
- Its headings are matched by the rule under [Heading anchors](#heading-anchors).
  It has no block anchors.
- No diagnostic is raised from its body.
- An embed of a Markdown card transcludes its body as CommonMark, with no
  HyperMarkDown expansion inside it.

**Why.** Its author wrote Markdown, so nothing in it is read as HyperMarkDown.

## Lexical model

HyperMarkDown constructs are found by **scanning**, not by fully parsing the
document as CommonMark. An implementation extracts only the constructs this
specification owns and leaves every other byte to whatever renders the document.

- Before extracting any construct, an implementation MUST **mask** these
  regions, so their contents never yield a link, an embed, or an anchor: fenced
  code blocks (both ` ``` ` and `~~~`), inline code spans, and HTML comments
  (`<!-- ... -->`). They are the only masked regions.
- Indented code blocks MUST NOT be masked.
- Raw inline HTML is not masked: a hand-written `<code>` element is scanned like
  any other text.
- Masking MUST preserve byte offsets, so that every construct carries an exact
  source span.
- Every construct MUST record a span of `(start_offset, end_offset, line,
  column)`. Offsets count UTF-8 bytes from the start of the card, starting at
  0. `line` and `column` start at 1, and `column` counts Unicode code points.
- Scanning MUST NOT depend on filesystem iteration order, on the contents of any
  other file, or on any state outside the card's own bytes. Everything
  cross-file belongs to resolution.

**Known divergence.** A line-oriented masking scanner differs from a full
CommonMark parser on pathological input, such as a wikilink inside an HTML
block or a fence opened inside a blockquote. This is permitted in 0.1. It is not
a licence to diverge on ordinary input.

**Why.** A four-space indent is also how a callout body and a footnote
continuation are written, so masking indented code would silently drop real
links from ordinary prose; an author who needs code has fences. Columns count
code points so that a position is the same in every implementation, whatever
its string type. The divergence is tolerated because the construct set is small
and every divergence can be diagnosed.

## Grammar

The constructs below are the format's own. Masked regions are the only way to
write a construct without invoking it.

```text
embed            := "!" wikilink
wikilink         := "[[" target [ "|" display ] "]]"
target           := page_ref [ fragment ]

page_ref         := absolute | relative | unqualified
absolute         := "/" segment { "/" segment }
relative         := "./" [ path_component { "/" path_component } ]
                  | "../" [ path_component { "/" path_component } ]
path_component   := segment | ".."
unqualified      := bare_name | unqualified_path
bare_name        := segment
unqualified_path := segment "/" segment { "/" segment }
segment          := 1*( any character except "/" or reserved )
reserved         := "[" | "]" | "|" | "#" | "^" | newline

fragment         := "#" heading_text
                  | "#^" block_id
heading_text     := heading_first { heading_char }
heading_first    := any character except "[" / "]" / "|" / "^" / newline
heading_char     := any character except "[" / "]" / "|" / newline
block_id         := ( ALPHA / DIGIT ) *63( ALPHA / DIGIT / "_" / "-" )

display          := 1*( any character except "[" / "]" / newline )
```

- A **bare name** is exactly one `segment`. A page reference with two or more
  segments and no leading path marker is an **unqualified path**. Both are
  **unqualified references** and use the same search; only a bare name can
  match a named import.
- The **reserved characters** inside a `target` are exactly `[`, `]`, `|`, `#`,
  `^`, and newline. They MUST NOT appear in a `segment`, and neither may `/`,
  the segment separator.
- A `heading_text` MUST contain at least one non-whitespace character. `#` and
  `^` MAY appear after its first character.
- `./` and `../` with no following component are valid references to the
  current and parent directory. They resolve only when that directory has a
  folder note, and the namespace containment rule still applies.
- A `page_ref` MAY end in a card extension. An implementation MUST strip it
  before matching and MUST then bind only that extension: `[[owl.md]]` reaches
  the Markdown card a HyperMarkDown card of the same name shadows.
- A `display` MUST NOT be empty. A wikilink with an empty display is malformed,
  an error ([HMD010](#diagnostics)).
- **No escape mechanism exists in this version.** A target that needs a reserved
  character is malformed, an error ([HMD010](#diagnostics)).
- The `namespace:path` form is reserved, not part of the 0.1 grammar; see
  [Not in this version](#not-in-this-version).

**Why.** `[`, `]`, and `|` delimit the wikilink, and `#` and `^` introduce its
fragment, so none of them can be part of a name. The missing escape is a known
gap, not a design position.

### Block anchors

A **block anchor** is a trailing `^block_id` at the end of a block's last line,
preceded by at least one space and followed only by the line ending.

- The anchor and its leading whitespace are not part of the block's content,
  and MUST NOT be rendered.
- An identifier's scope is its card. Two cards MAY use the same identifier; two
  anchors in one card MUST NOT, and a duplicate is an error ([HMD006](#diagnostics)).
- An anchor at the end of a heading's line names that heading. Its block is the
  heading's whole section; see [Embed expansion](#embed-expansion).

### Frontmatter block

A card MAY open with a frontmatter block: a `---` fence that MUST begin at byte
0 of the card and MUST close with a `---` line.

- The block MUST parse as YAML and MUST be a mapping at the top level.
  Otherwise it is an error ([HMD009](#diagnostics)).
- Exactly four top-level keys are **reserved**: `tags`, `use`, `import`, and
  `nav`, specified under [Frontmatter](#frontmatter).
- Every other key is the author's and MUST NOT be interpreted. An implementation
  MUST still expose the whole mapping to its consumers.

**Why.** The language ships mechanisms, not vocabulary: the four keys it needs,
and nothing that would collide with an author's own.

## Heading anchors

A heading fragment names a heading by its text. Both sides are normalised
before they are compared, so a link need not repeat a heading's capitalisation
or punctuation.

- A **heading** is an ATX heading (`# Setup`) or a setext heading (`Setup`
  over a line of `===` or `---`), as CommonMark defines them. Both are
  addressable. A line inside a masked region is never a heading.
- A heading's **text** is its content as a reader sees it, with inline markup
  removed: `# The *fast* path` has the text `The fast path`. A closing run of
  `#` and a trailing block anchor are not part of it.
- The **key** of a text, a heading's or a fragment's, is computed in four steps:

```text
key(text):
  1. normalise to Unicode NFC
  2. apply Unicode full case folding
  3. replace every character that is not a letter, a mark, or a number
     (general categories L, M, N) with a space
  4. collapse every run of spaces into one, and trim both ends
```

- A `heading_text` fragment MUST match the first heading, in document order,
  whose key equals the fragment's key. A fragment that matches no heading is an
  error ([HMD004](#diagnostics)).
- Letters and digits of every script are kept. `Ошибка 404`, `Café`, and `数据`
  each have a key of their own, and accents are significant.
- A heading whose key equals that of an earlier heading in the same card MUST
  be reported as a warning, unless it carries a block anchor
  ([HMD011](#diagnostics)). A fragment reaches the first of them; the anchor,
  `## Linux ^setup-linux`, linked as `#^setup-linux`, reaches the later one.
  Adding the anchor is the remedy, so it ends the warning.
- A heading whose key is empty, such as one made only of symbols, cannot be
  reached by its text. It can be reached by an anchor.
- How a renderer turns a heading into an HTML `id`, or a fragment into a URL, is
  not part of the language. Whatever it chooses, it MUST rewrite each heading
  link to the `id` it gave the heading that link matched.

In the example vault, `[[setup#linux]]`, `[[setup#Linux]]`, and
`[[setup#LINUX!]]` all reach `## Linux`.

**Why.** Link by heading text where the link should read naturally; anchor a
heading whose links must survive a rename, or whose text is awkward to type.
The matching agrees with Obsidian's, which also ignores case and punctuation.
The specification states the key itself rather than naming a library, so no
implementation has to copy another's behaviour.

## The namespace

The namespace is a single rooted subtree. In version 0.1 nothing outside it is
addressable by a page reference. Ordinary Markdown links still address files
and URLs outside it.

- The **vault** is the nearest ancestor directory that contains a `.hmd/`
  directory. Nothing else marks one; a `.git` directory does not. A tree with
  no `.hmd/` above it has no vault, and its namespace root has to be named
  directly.
- The **namespace root** is the directory the `wiki` setting of
  `.hmd/config.toml` names, relative to the vault, defaulting to `doc/wiki`.
  `wiki = "."` makes the vault itself the namespace root.
- A file is a card if and only if it lies below the namespace root and has a
  card extension. A card's path is written relative to the namespace root.
- An implementation MUST offer a way to name the namespace root directly,
  overriding both.
- A resolved target MUST lie inside the namespace root. One that does not is
  an error ([HMD003](#diagnostics)).
- Path normalisation MUST happen before that containment check, and symbolic
  links MUST NOT be followed out of the root.
- A directory below the namespace root that contains its own `.hmd/` is another
  vault. No card in its subtree is part of the outer namespace, whether as a
  page, a resolution candidate, or an import target, and references do not
  cross that boundary in either direction.

A directory inside the namespace is a **module**: the unit that scopes a name
and inherits configuration. A directory MAY carry a **folder note**, a card
named `index` with any card extension. A reference to the directory and a
reference to its explicit `index` path resolve to that one card.
Configuration inherits from `index.hmd` only; a Markdown folder note's
frontmatter is not interpreted, so it sets nothing for its subtree.

In the example vault, `handbook/` is the vault, `wiki/` is the namespace root,
and `[[guides]]` reaches `guides/index.hmd`.

**Why.** One marker has one meaning: a tree inside a git repository is not a
vault until it says so.

## Name resolution

A `page_ref` is a local card reference. An absolute or relative path is followed
directly. An unqualified reference is searched for, and resolves to exactly one
card or to a failure that can be diagnosed.

The search for an unqualified reference checks named imports first, then walks
the **spine** from the card outward to the root, then probes imported search
paths, and only if all of those find nothing runs one **sweep** of the tree.

**Why.** The spine answers *what does this reference mean here*, which must be
predictable enough to hold in your head. The sweep answers *does this reference
exist anywhere*, which is a convenience and is allowed to fail loudly.

### Binding an unqualified reference in one directory

Every search phase binds a reference in a directory the same way, so a folder
note behaves the same everywhere.

```text
bind(dir, parts, ext?) -> Resolved(path) | NotFound

  exts := [ext] if the reference wrote one, else [".hmd", ".md", ".markdown"]

  for e in exts:
      file := dir / join(parts) + e
      if file exists:                      return Resolved(file)
  for e in exts:
      note := dir / join(parts) / ("index" + e)
      if note exists:                      return Resolved(note)
  return NotFound
```

- A target naming a directory MUST resolve to that directory's folder note, so
  the directory form and the explicit `index` form reach the same card.
- Two files in one directory whose names differ only in their card extension
  claim one name. The one with the earliest extension is the card at that name;
  every other is **shadowed**: still a card, never bound by a reference that
  writes no extension, bound by one that writes its extension, and never a
  sweep candidate. Each shadowed file MUST be reported once, as a
  warning at its line 1, column 1 ([HMD018](#diagnostics)). Folder notes shadow one another the same
  way.
- If a card and a folder note claim the same name as siblings, the pair MUST be
  reported as an error ([HMD012](#diagnostics)). After that error an implementation MUST select the file
  card, so that diagnostics and rendering stay deterministic; the selection does
  not make the collision valid.

### The algorithm

```text
resolve(target, source, root) -> Resolved(path) | Unresolved | Ambiguous(paths) | Escapes

  ext   := the card extension target.page_ref ends in, if any
  stem  := target.page_ref with ext removed
  parts := stem split on "/"
  bindings := named_imports(source)                      # name -> card
  paths    := wildcard_imports(source)                   # ordered dirs
  mode     := discovery_mode(source)                     # spine | both | recursive

  1. ABSOLUTE — stem starts with "/"
       return bind(root, parts) or Unresolved

  2. RELATIVE — stem starts with "./" or "../"
       base := normalize(dirname(source) / stem)
       if base is outside root: return Escapes
       return bind(dirname(base), [basename(base)]) or Unresolved

  3. UNQUALIFIED — anything else

       # Phase 0 — named imports. The most explicit thing wins.
       if size(parts) == 1 and parts[0] in bindings:
           return Resolved(bindings[parts[0]])

       # Phase 1 — spine walk. Non-recursive, nearest first.
       spine := [dirname(source), parent(dirname(source)), ..., root]
       for dir in spine:
           r := bind(dir, parts)
           if r is Resolved: return r

       # Phase 2 — imported search paths, in declaration order.
       for dir in paths:
           r := bind(dir, parts)
           if r is Resolved: return r

       if mode == "spine": return Unresolved      # autodiscovery off

       # Phase 3 — autodiscovery. Runs at most once.
       # Every card, less shadowed files; only `ext` when one was written.
       if mode == "recursive":
           candidates := cards_below(dirname(source), ext)
       else:                                      # mode == "both"
           candidates := cards_below(root, ext)
       matches := { p in candidates
                    : addressable_parts(p) ends with parts }
       if size(matches) == 1: return Resolved(the match)
       if size(matches) >  1: return Ambiguous(sorted(matches))
       return Unresolved
```

`discovery_mode(source)` is `spine` when autodiscovery is off for the source
card, by its effective `use` toggle, and otherwise the project's `mode`.
`addressable_parts(p)` is `p`'s root-relative parts without its card extension,
plus — when `p` is a folder note — the parts of its directory, so that an
unqualified reference means the same thing in the sweep as on the spine.

In the example vault, `[[tokens]]` in `guides/install.hmd` stops at Phase 1 in
`guides/`. The same reference in `setup.hmd` reaches Phase 3 and is ambiguous.

### Rules the algorithm must satisfy

- Resolution MUST follow the phase order above. A named import MAY shadow a
  local card. A wildcard import MUST NOT override a named import or a spine
  match, but adding one MAY change a reference that previously resolved through
  the sweep.
- The spine MUST run from the card's own directory outward to the namespace
  root, inclusive of both ends, and each directory MUST be probed
  **non-recursively**. The spine never searches sideways into a sibling module.
- Imported search paths MUST be probed in declaration order, non-recursively.
  Where two of them hold the name, the earlier declaration wins, and the
  shadowed candidate MUST be reported as a warning ([HMD016](#diagnostics)).
- The sweep MUST run only when the earlier phases found nothing, and MUST run at
  most once. When autodiscovery is on, it MAY resolve a unique match in a
  sibling module or anywhere else in its scope.
- The sweep MUST address folder notes by their directory name, exactly as
  binding does.
- In the sweep **all matches rank equally**. A shallower match MUST NOT beat a
  deeper one, and more than one match MUST be reported as an error rather than
  resolved ([HMD002](#diagnostics)).
- Candidate lists MUST be sorted by root-relative POSIX path.
- An unresolved target is **not** a failure. It is a forward reference, reported
  as a warning ([HMD001](#diagnostics)).

**Why.** Every match the sweep produces is off-spine and off-path by
construction, so the phases can never disagree. The remedy for an ambiguity is
to qualify the link, not to memorise a tie-break. An earlier import silently
winning is easy to write by accident, hence the warning. Sorted candidates make a
diagnostic byte-identical across platforms. Writing forward is how a knowledge
base grows.

### Fragments

Once a card is resolved, its fragment is matched against that card.

- A `heading_text` fragment MUST match by the rule under
  [Heading anchors](#heading-anchors); no match is an error ([HMD004](#diagnostics)).
- A `block_id` fragment MUST match a block anchor in the resolved card; no match
  is an error ([HMD005](#diagnostics)).
- A fragment MUST NOT influence which card is resolved.

## Frontmatter

Configuration has two levels. A card says **whether** a feature applies to it;
project configuration says **how** that feature behaves. The order of the
resolution phases is the same everywhere; a card may only turn the sweep off.

```yaml
---
tags: [area/auth, status/accepted]
use: [no_autodiscovery]
import:
  - from /shared import tokens as shared-tokens
  - from ../billing import invoices
  - from /glossary import *
nav:
  order: 10
  visibility: public
---
```

### `tags` — what a card is about

- `tags` is a list of strings. A `/` separates hierarchy levels, so `area/backend`
  is a descendant of `area`. A malformed value is an error ([HMD013](#diagnostics)).
- Tags MUST NOT take part in resolution. A tag is not a link target, and a
  target beginning with `#` is malformed ([HMD010](#diagnostics)).
- What tags mean beyond their shape — tag pages, hierarchy queries — is not
  specified in this version.

**Why.** A wikilink answers *where a card lives*; a tag answers *what it is
about*. Collapsing the two breaks both. Only the slot is fixed, so authors can
tag against a stable shape now.

### `use` — per-card feature toggles

- `use` accepts a string or a list of strings. Each entry names a feature;
  prefixing `no_` disables it. A feature not mentioned takes its project
  default.
- The only feature this version defines is **`autodiscovery`**: whether the
  sweep runs at all.
- **`use` inherits.** A `use` list in a folder note applies to that directory's
  whole subtree; the nearest declaration wins, and a card overrides its
  directory.
- An unrecognised feature name MUST be reported as an error, not ignored
  ([HMD013](#diagnostics)).

**Why.** With the sweep off, every dependency a card has is visible in its
header or in the link itself. A dropped misspelling would hand the author the
default while they believe they configured something else.

### `import` — explicit name binding

`import` is a list of one-line statements in the grammar below, not nested YAML.

```text
import_stmt := "from" WS ref WS "import" WS import_list
import_list := "*" | binding *( "," WS binding )
binding     := name [ WS "as" WS alias ]
ref         := absolute | relative          ; never unqualified
name        := segment
alias       := segment
```

- **A named binding** binds one name: the alias if given, otherwise the
  imported name, resolves to that one card and to nothing else. It applies only
  to bare names, so a multi-segment target falls through it.
- **A wildcard** adds a search origin: the directory joins the paths an
  unqualified reference is probed against, after the spine. It does not bind the
  directory's contents eagerly, so a card added there later is reachable without
  editing the importing card.
- A `ref` MUST be absolute or relative, never unqualified.
- An imported origin MUST be probed non-recursively.
- Two named bindings of the same local name MUST be reported as an error
  ([HMD015](#diagnostics)).
- An import whose `ref` does not exist MUST be reported as an error at the
  declaration, not at first use ([HMD015](#diagnostics)).
- A malformed statement MUST be reported as an error ([HMD014](#diagnostics)).
- **`import` does not inherit.** An import in a folder note binds names for that
  file alone.

**Why.** An unqualified `ref` would need resolving by the very algorithm the
import feeds. Probing without recursion keeps the reachable set legible from a
directory listing. Two bindings of one name have no declaration order to appeal
to, and last-wins is guessing. A wrong import is a defect in the header whether
or not the body uses it. Inherited imports would make a leaf card's reference
resolvable only by reading a file its author may never have opened.

### `nav` — placement and publication

`nav` is a mapping with two keys, `order` and `visibility`.

- **`order`** is an integer. Cards that set it sort ahead of cards that do not,
  ascending; ties fall back to the default order: folder notes first, then cards
  by root-relative POSIX path.
- **`visibility`** is `public` or `private`, and gates publication. Any other
  value MUST be reported as an error, not coerced ([HMD013](#diagnostics)).
- Publication is **opt-in**. A card reaches a published site only if its
  effective visibility is `public`; the default is `private`.
- An unpublished card MUST NOT be registered at all: no page, no address.
- A Markdown card's effective visibility is `public`, and a publisher MUST NOT
  register it as a page of its own: it is published where its file is, by
  whatever publishes Markdown. A link or an embed to one points at the page the
  site builds from that file, and renders as an unresolved link where the site
  builds none.
- `visibility` **inherits** the way `use` does: the card's own value, then the
  nearest ancestor folder note that sets one, then the default. `public` on the
  root folder note therefore publishes the whole namespace, and a card opts out
  with its own `private`.
- A published card that links to an unpublished one MUST render the link as
  unresolved, and MUST be reported as a warning ([HMD017](#diagnostics)).
- A published card that **embeds** an unpublished one MUST NOT expand it. It
  degrades to the same unresolved link and the same warning. Expansion itself
  takes the publication predicate from its caller.
- A scalar `nav`, an unknown key inside it, or a non-integer `order` MUST be
  reported as an error ([HMD013](#diagnostics)).

In the example vault, `index.hmd` is `public`, so every card is published.

**Why.** `nav` is a mapping so that a further placement key can be added without
a second spelling for the first; a scalar `nav` asked to be ordered and would
silently sort last. Publication is opt-in because the two mistakes are not
symmetric: a card that should have shipped and did not is visible to its author
on the next build, while one that shipped and should not have is a leak nobody
looks for. A page reachable by typing its address is published, whatever the
sidebar says. Copying a private card's bytes into a public page is the one
outcome the gate exists to prevent. Rendering a card named directly is not the
same situation as building a site, so expansion stays free of policy.

## Project configuration

The `.hmd/` directory marks the vault and holds its configuration. This version
reads exactly three settings from `.hmd/config.toml` — `wiki`, `autodiscovery`,
and `mode` — and MUST ignore the rest of the file.

```toml
wiki = "doc/wiki"        # the namespace root, relative to the vault

[discovery]
autodiscovery = true     # whether the sweep runs at all
mode = "both"            # both (default) | recursive — how the sweep searches
```

- **`wiki`** names the namespace root, relative to the vault. It moves where the
  cards start and nothing else: `.hmd/` stays in the vault.
- **`autodiscovery`** is the project default for the `use` feature of the same
  name. A card overrides it in either direction.
- **`mode`** decides how the sweep searches: `both` sweeps the whole namespace;
  `recursive` sweeps only the source card's own subtree.
- `mode` MUST NOT be settable per card. A card MAY turn autodiscovery off, but
  MUST NOT change the scope or ranking of the sweep.
- Without `.hmd/config.toml`, the namespace root is `doc/wiki`, autodiscovery is
  on, and the mode is `both`.

Precedence, most specific first:

```text
1. the card's own `use`
2. `use` in the nearest ancestor folder note
3. [discovery] autodiscovery in .hmd/config.toml
4. the built-in default — autodiscovery is ON
```

**Why.** Ignoring unknown settings lets the full schema be specified later
without invalidating existing trees. Frontmatter beats configuration because the
card is the most local place an author can say what they mean. With no
configuration at all, bare names find cards, which is what a new author expects.

## Embed expansion

An **embed** is any wikilink with a `!` in front. It is resolved by the
algorithm above and then expanded in place.

- An embed of a whole card MUST expand to the target's full body with its
  frontmatter block removed.
- An embed of a heading fragment MUST expand to the matched heading and every
  following line up to, but excluding, the next heading of the same or higher
  level.
- An embed of a block fragment MUST expand to the anchored block with the
  trailing anchor stripped. When the anchor is on a heading's line, the block is
  that heading's section, exactly as for a heading fragment.
- Expansion MUST be recursive: embeds inside expanded content are expanded too.
- A link or embed inside expanded content MUST be resolved as if it were read
  in the card it was written in: from that card's directory, with that card's
  imports and its own `use` toggles.
- An implementation MUST keep a stack of resolved `(path, fragment)` pairs.
  Re-entering a pair already on the stack is a cycle, an error ([HMD007](#diagnostics)).
- The maximum expansion depth is **16**. Exceeding it is an error ([HMD008](#diagnostics)).
- Expansion MUST NOT shift heading levels: they arrive exactly as the source
  wrote them.

In the example vault, `![[setup#^setup-linux]]` and `![[setup#Linux]]` expand to
the same text: the `## Linux` heading and its paragraph, up to `## macOS`.

**Why.** Embedding a card must never change what its links mean. An anchor on a
heading is that heading's stable name, so it embeds what the heading embeds.
Sixteen levels are deep enough for real composition and shallow enough to bound
work on adversarial input. Leaving level shifting out keeps expansion textual
and reversible.

## Diagnostics

Rule identifiers are stable and MUST NOT be renumbered once released. This
version has no way to silence a diagnostic.

| ID | Severity | Condition | Defined in |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| HMD001 | warning | Link target does not resolve | [Rules](#rules-the-algorithm-must-satisfy) |
| HMD002 | error | An unqualified reference matches more than one card in the sweep | [Rules](#rules-the-algorithm-must-satisfy) |
| HMD003 | error | A target resolves outside the namespace root | [The namespace](#the-namespace) |
| HMD004 | error | A heading fragment is not present in the resolved card | [Heading anchors](#heading-anchors) |
| HMD005 | error | A block identifier is not present in the resolved card | [Fragments](#fragments) |
| HMD006 | error | Duplicate block anchor identifier within one card | [Block anchors](#block-anchors) |
| HMD007 | error | Embed cycle | [Embed expansion](#embed-expansion) |
| HMD008 | error | Embed depth limit (16) exceeded | [Embed expansion](#embed-expansion) |
| HMD009 | error | Frontmatter is not valid YAML, or not a top-level mapping | [Frontmatter block](#frontmatter-block) |
| HMD010 | error | Malformed wikilink or embed syntax | [Grammar](#grammar) |
| HMD011 | warning | A heading has the same key as an earlier one in the card, and no block anchor | [Heading anchors](#heading-anchors) |
| HMD012 | error | A card and a folder note claim the same name | [Binding](#binding-an-unqualified-reference-in-one-directory) |
| HMD013 | error | Unrecognised `use` feature, or a malformed `tags` or `nav` value | [`tags`](#tags-what-a-card-is-about), [`use`](#use-per-card-feature-toggles), [`nav`](#nav-placement-and-publication) |
| HMD014 | error | Malformed `import` statement | [`import`](#import-explicit-name-binding) |
| HMD015 | error | An import `ref` does not resolve, or two named imports bind one local name | [`import`](#import-explicit-name-binding) |
| HMD016 | warning | An unqualified reference matched two imported search paths; the earlier won | [Rules](#rules-the-algorithm-must-satisfy) |
| HMD017 | warning | A published card links to or embeds one that is not published | [`nav`](#nav-placement-and-publication) |
| HMD018 | warning | A card shadows a card of the same name with a later card extension | [Binding](#binding-an-unqualified-reference-in-one-directory) |
| HMD019 | error | A file with a card extension is not valid UTF-8 | [Encoding](#encoding) |

- Each diagnostic MUST carry the rule identifier, the severity, a root-relative
  path with POSIX separators, a line, a column, and a message. An ambiguity MUST
  also carry its sorted candidate list.
- Diagnostics MUST be emitted sorted by path, then line, then column, then rule.
- Strict mode MUST NOT alter diagnostic records or their severity. For
  aggregate counts and for command success or failure only, it MUST count every
  warning as an error.
- Nothing beyond these rules is checked. The language checks the graph, the
  frontmatter, and the encoding, and makes no claim about prose, style, or
  inherited Markdown.

**Why.** CI configuration and documentation cite rule identifiers, so they never
move. An unresolved reference is a warning because writing forward is
permitted, and the report is a to-do list. Several sweep matches are an error
because the sweep has no ranking rule; imported search paths do have an order,
so a shadowed match there is only a warning. A strict run and an ordinary run
over the same tree contain the same diagnostics and differ only in their summary
and outcome.

## Rendering

The language does not specify a renderer. Two constraints are nonetheless
normative, because a renderer that breaks them changes what the document means:

- A target that does not resolve MUST still render as a link, marked as
  unresolved, rather than being dropped or raising an error.
- Embed expansion MUST follow [Embed expansion](#embed-expansion), including the
  depth and cycle bounds, whatever the output format.

An implementation MAY erase the graph on output — resolving every link to a path
and expanding every embed — producing ordinary Markdown or HTML that a reader
can use without knowing the format exists. Erasure is lossy: the boundary
between a card and the content it embedded is gone, and so is the source of
each link.

**Why.** A missing card is a work item; erasing the author's reference hides
it. Erasure is right for shipping and wrong for anything that must show a reader
where content came from.

## Cells

A **cell** is a fenced code block that a tool runs or draws. Its kind is the
first word of the fence's info string, and the cell kinds are exactly:

| Kind | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `hql#eval` | an [HQL](https://hql-lang.org/) query whose output is saved in the card |
| `hql#query` | an HQL query that runs for the cells that read it, and keeps no output |
| `d2#show` | a diagram in D2 |
| `mermaid#show` | a diagram in Mermaid |

- Kinds MUST be matched exactly and case-sensitively. A fence of any other kind
  is an ordinary fenced block.
- A plain `hql`, `d2`, or `mermaid` fence is a **listing**: source shown as
  code, never run and never drawn.
- A cell's body is masked like that of every fenced block. It yields no link,
  no embed, no anchor, and no edge in the graph.
- An `hql#eval` cell MAY be followed by its saved output, between an
  `<!-- hql:output … -->` and an `<!-- /hql:output -->` marker. The output is
  ordinary card content: a link in it is a link like any other. A card with
  cells and saved outputs is a well-formed card.
- The syntax inside a cell, its attributes, the output markers, and the hash
  that tells a current output from a stale one are HQL's, and are specified
  there.

*Suggested presentation, not a rule.* Show a cell's output or drawing first,
with its source behind a toggle. Run an HQL cell only when the reader asks, and
write its output into the card. Draw a diagram cell when the card opens and
again when the reader asks, and never write the drawing into the card.

**Why.** A listing is how a card shows such source without invoking it.

## Extended Markdown syntax

HyperMarkDown extends CommonMark 0.31.2 with the
following language constructs. Their syntax and semantics
are based on the corresponding specifications referenced below.

| Construct | Syntax | Reference |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Tables | pipe-delimited rows, with a delimiter row under the header | GitHub Flavored Markdown |
| Task lists | `- [ ] open` and `- [x] done` | GitHub Flavored Markdown |
| Strikethrough | `~~text~~` | GitHub Flavored Markdown |
| Subscript | `~text~` | PyMdown Extensions |
| Footnotes | `text[^1]`, with `[^1]: the note` elsewhere | PHP Markdown Extra |
| Callouts | `!!! note "Title"`, then a body indented four spaces | Python-Markdown |
| Collapsible callouts | `??? note` closed, `???+ note` open | PyMdown Extensions |
| Maths | `$…$` inline, `$$…$$` on lines of its own | TeX |
| Attribute lists | `{: .class }` after a block | Python-Markdown |
| Markdown inside HTML | `<div markdown>` | Python-Markdown |
| Drawn diagrams | a `d2#show` or `mermaid#show` cell | D2, Mermaid |

## Not in this version

The following are **deferred**, specified nowhere yet and not to be inferred
from their absence: inline properties, highlights, templates and parameterised
transclusion, backlinks and generated category pages, and heading-level
shifting on embed. Querying the document graph is
[HQL](https://hql-lang.org/)'s, a language of its own; a card carries it in
cells.

The following is **reserved**: a cross-namespace address of the form
`namespace:path/to/card`. The spelling is claimed so that it cannot be given
another meaning, and an implementation of this version MUST treat it as a
malformed target.

The gap an author is most likely to meet is the absence of an **escape
mechanism**, in both directions. A target cannot contain a reserved character,
and the only way to show a construct without invoking it is to put it in a code
span or a fenced block. Raw inline HTML is not masked and is therefore not a
workaround.

